<section>
  <h2 id="read-a-notification">The relay accepted it. What happened next?</h2>
  {{> components/pattern-examples topic="delivery-reports"}}
  <p>Your billing job submits an invoice on Friday. On Monday a bounce lands in the return mailbox. The receipt records what the
    submission server accepted on Friday. Convert the notification to an <code>Email</code> to see what the reporting server says happened next.
    One report can contain an outcome for each of several addresses.</p>
  <p>Look for the machine-readable status part alongside the explanation a person sees. Simple Java Mail reads that part. Text such as
    “could not deliver” on its own gives your application an ordinary email.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-text">multipart/report; report-type=delivery-status
├── Human-readable explanation
├── message/delivery-status: recipient actions, status codes and diagnostics
└── Original message or its headers (optional)</code></pre>
  <pre><code class="language-java">Email notification = mail.converter()
    .fromMimeMessage(incomingMessage)
    .toEmail();

DeliveryStatusReport report = notification.getDeliveryStatusReport();
if (report != null) {
    System.out.println(report);
    for (RecipientDeliveryReport recipient : report.getRecipients()) {
        System.out.println(recipient.getAction() + " " + recipient.getStatusCode());
    }
}</code></pre>
  <p>You might see this for a notification containing three recipient reports:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-text">DeliveryStatusReport{recipients=3, delivered=1, delayed=1, failed=1, warnings=0}
FAILED 5.1.1
DELIVERED 2.0.0
DELAYED 4.4.1</code></pre>
  <p>Feed the message from your bounce handler into the same EML or MIME converter you use for other received mail. Supply receive keys there
    if needed. <code>getDeliveryStatusReport()</code> reads the immutable result of that conversion, so your handler can call it without further I/O.</p>
</section>
<section>
  <h2 id="correlate-and-decide">Match the notification, then make your decision</h2>
  <p>The invoice bounce contains <code>Original-Envelope-Id</code>. After checking where it came from, compare that value to the envelope
    ID saved from the job’s <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html">submission receipt</a>. Then check the action for each recipient.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-java">String reportedId = report.getEnvelopeId();
boolean matches = reportedId != null &amp;&amp; reportedId.equals(receipt.getEnvelopeId());</code></pre>
  <p>Compare the ID exactly, including trailing spaces. The parser strips leading field whitespace. Conflicting repeated IDs leave the ID
    unavailable. Store it separately from Message-ID, progress observation IDs and telemetry trace IDs.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>FAILED 5.1.1</code></strong> &mdash; flag the mailbox for correction. The reporting server has stopped trying.</li>
    <li><strong><code>DELAYED 4.4.1</code></strong> &mdash; keep the recipient pending. A later <code>FAILED 4.4.1</code> means the server gave up.</li>
    <li><strong><code>DELIVERED 2.0.0</code></strong> &mdash; record reported delivery for this address. A read by the recipient is a separate event.</li>
    <li><strong><code>RELAYED</code> or <code>EXPANDED</code></strong> &mdash; record the handoff or expansion. Look to later reports for final mailbox evidence.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Read the action and status together. A retry-until timestamp describes the reporting server’s schedule. Your application decides whether to resend.</p>
  <details class="doc-disclosure">
    <summary>Read the status code and SMTP reply</summary>
    <pre><code class="language-java">for (RecipientDeliveryReport recipient : report.getRecipients()) {
    EnhancedStatusCode status = recipient.getStatusCode();
    if (status != null) {
        System.out.println(status + ": permanent=" + status.isPermanentFailure());
    }
    DeliveryDiagnostic diagnostic = recipient.getDiagnostic();
    if (diagnostic != null &amp;&amp; diagnostic.getSmtpResponse() != null) {
        System.out.println("SMTP reply: " + diagnostic.getSmtpResponse().getReturnCode());
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>For a permanent mailbox failure this can print <code>5.1.1: permanent=true</code> and <code>SMTP reply: 550</code>.
      The status class, subject and detail are numeric even for unfamiliar combinations. A diagnostic retains its original
      <code>getType()</code> and <code>getText()</code>. Only a recognizable SMTP reply supplies a parsed <code>SmtpServerResponse</code>.</p>
  </details>
  <details class="doc-disclosure">
    <summary>Identify the recipient and reporting server</summary>
    <p>Each entry carries original and final <code>ReportedRecipient</code> fields, a <code>DeliveryAction</code>, status, optional diagnostic,
      and any last-attempt or retry-until timestamps. Addresses keep their reported format, spelling and duplicates.
      A <code>ReportedMailServer</code> pairs the reporting server’s name with its naming format.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-java">for (RecipientDeliveryReport recipient : report.getRecipients()) {
    ReportedRecipient target = recipient.getFinalRecipient();
    if (target != null) {
        Recipient mailbox = target.getMailbox();
        if (mailbox != null) {
            // Addresses can identify people. Choose deliberately what you log.
            System.out.println(mailbox.getAddress());
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p><code>getMailbox()</code> parses a recognized Internet address. The name is usually null. Parsing performs no lookup and adds no
      recipient role or certificate. For other formats such as <code>x400</code>, malformed addresses or lists, read the reported
      <code>getType()</code> and <code>getAddress()</code>.</p>
  </details>
</section>
<section>
  <h2 id="imperfect-notifications">Read what you can from an imperfect notification</h2>
  <p>Say the invoice notification’s stream fails while you’re reading the second recipient. The first recipient’s completed block
    remains available. Check <code>report.getWarnings()</code> before treating that one result as the whole report.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-text">Original-Envelope-Id: order-123
Reporting-MTA: dns; relay.example.org

Final-Recipient: rfc822; alice@example.org
Action: failed
Status: 5.1.1

Final-Recipient: rfc822; bob@example.org
[Read fails here, before the second block ends.]</code></pre>
  <p>You can match <code>order-123</code> to the job and act on the first address. Leave the second address unresolved.
    The warning says the recovered facts may be incomplete.</p>
  <details class="doc-disclosure">
    <summary>Which fields survive unusual report formatting</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>Simple Java Mail can recover a numeric status from <code>5.01.001</code> or read an explicit ISO timestamp such as
        <code>2026-10-16T10:20:30+02:00</code>. Each recovery adds a warning and leaves the original message intact.</li>
      <li>A valid mail date can end with a comment, as in <code>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 10:20:30 +0200 (CEST)</code>, without a recovery warning.
        Malformed comments, extra text, impossible dates and missing or invalid timezones leave the timestamp unavailable.
        Numeric timezone hours must be 00 through 23, so <code>+2400</code> and <code>+9900</code> are invalid.</li>
      <li><code>Action: failed (recipient removed)</code> yields <code>FAILED</code> without a warning. Action names are case-insensitive
        and allow valid comments before or after the name. Extra tokens, quoted values, malformed comments and unrecognized actions yield <code>UNKNOWN</code>.</li>
      <li>The parser can recognize a lone direct status part even in the wrong position. Two competing parts leave the report facts
        unavailable. Attached or nested notifications have separate report data.</li>
      <li>Missing or unusable scalar fields are <code>null</code>. Recipient and warning lists are immutable and never null.
        A recognized malformed report retains usable facts and warnings without picking among contradictions.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>
  <details class="doc-disclosure">
    <summary>Partial reads, close failures and source inspection</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>After a read failure, completed header blocks remain available. An unfinished block is not presented as complete.
        The source data retains the bytes already acquired before a failed read. <code>isFullyCaptured()</code> is <code>false</code>,
        and a Problem links to the affected part.</li>
      <li>If the stream fails to close after a complete read, the source keeps the complete capture and the Problem records the close failure.
        Simple Java Mail reads the original selected part even if attachments are deferred. It does not fetch another copy for diagnostics or turn a partial attachment into complete content.</li>
      <li>Interpretation warnings in <code>email.getProblems()</code> link to affected parts via <code>problem.getSources()</code>.
        An ambiguous report can point to the known conflicting parts. A missing status part can point to its container.
        Follow those links in <a href="/email-conversion.html#section-source-inspection">received-source inspection</a> without parsing a
        location string or reading the payload again.</li>
      <li>Cancellation remains a failure when acquiring, reading or closing the report stream. A typed interruption can occur without the
        thread’s interrupt flag being set. Ordinary I/O failures and socket timeouts allow the recovery described above. Application failures
        keep their own exceptions.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>
</section>
<section>
  <h2 id="trust-and-preservation">Decide whether to act on the bounce</h2>
  <p>Your invoice worker saved the envelope ID from its submission receipt. When a failure notice comes in, check the receiving route
    and match the ID before changing the job.</p>
  <dl>
    <dt><strong>Trusted route and a matching send</strong></dt>
    <dd>Apply the recipient outcome to that invoice once. A repeated or older report should follow the same rules as other job events.
      For example, an older delay should leave a recorded permanent failure in place.</dd>
    <dt><strong>Untrusted route or an ID you cannot match</strong></dt>
    <dd>Hold the report for review. A forged notice could otherwise suppress a real customer’s address or trigger another invoice.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>A missing report leaves delivery unknown. A <code>DELIVERED</code> outcome records what the reporting system says about delivery.
    Reading the message is a separate event.</p>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>When a converted message exposes a report</summary>
<p>Ordinary messages return <code>null</code>. A report attached to an ordinary message is not promoted into its parent's report.
    Existing protection processing runs first: readable signed/decrypted notifications can expose reports, while inaccessible ciphertext cannot.
    Inspect the protection results too; a null report does not establish that encrypted content contains none.</p>
</details>
<details class="doc-disclosure">
  <summary>Keeping, exporting and logging report data</summary>
<ul>
    <li>Keep the received <code>Email</code> when you want the historical report. Ordinary copies and supported Java serialization keep
      that snapshot through content edits.</li>
    <li>Defaults and overrides resolve outgoing content and settings. New replies, forwards and completed messages start without the
      received report or its nested facts.</li>
    <li>Exact EML inspection preserves the source bytes and performs no added decryption. Exporting a copied <code>Email</code>
      to MIME also leaves report generation to your application or mail system.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A received <code>DeliveryStatusReport</code> is separate from <code>DeliveryStatusNotification</code>, which configures a request for future
    feedback on an outgoing submission. Defaults and overrides can resolve that outgoing request; the received report is historical input data.</p>
  <p>Automatic report rendering includes counts, not addresses, identifiers or diagnostic prose. If you log or export those getters, treat the values
    as untrusted and potentially personal data. JSON/YAML output belongs to your application's normal serializer and DTOs; Simple Java Mail adds no serialization dependency.</p>
</details>
</section>
<section>
  <h2 id="teach-the-integration">Connect facts to your application.</h2>
  <p>Simple Java Mail interprets mail. Your job runner, broker or workflow engine owns persistence, scheduling and business transitions.
    Explore <a href="/strategic-patterns.html">Strategic Patterns</a> for runnable Docker applications, or the
    <a href="/email-workload-field-guide.html#the-invoice-was-accepted-then-bounced">Tactical Pattern for an accepted invoice that later bounces</a>.</p>
  <p>Delivery-status parsing follows <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3464">RFC 3464</a> and
    <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6533">RFC 6533</a>. Human-only bounce messages without a machine-readable report remain ordinary Email content.</p>
</section>
